"1", "lp": -0.00011778571206377819, "top": [["1", -0.00011778571206377819], ["0", -9.13407039642334]]}, {"t": " and", "lp": -0.1883847713470459, "top": [[" and", -0.1883847713470459], [" or", -1.9374291896820068]]}, {"t": " ", "lp": -3.6240282497601584e-05, "top": [[" ", -3.6240282497601584e-05], [" are", -11.972147941589355]]}], "text": "Based on current technical documentation and driver behavior for Intel Arc GPUs (including the A770) under the Linux `xe` driver (or the newer `intel-mesa`/`i915` drivers), here is the breakdown:\n\n### 1. **CCS Mode 1 vs. Mode 2**\n\n**CCS (Constant Color Surface)** modes refer to how the GPU handles **color space conversions** and **gamma correction** during rendering, particularly when using **YUV** or **HDR** content.\n\n- **CCS Mode 1**:  \n  - **Standard sRGB / Linear workflow**.  \n  - Used for **SDR (Standard Dynamic Range)** content.  \n  - Applies **gamma correction** (typically 2.2) when converting from linear to sRGB.  \n  - **Default mode** for most desktop compositing and video playback.  \n  - **Use when**: You are displaying standard SDR content (e.g., YouTube videos, desktop UI, games in SDR mode).\n\n- **CCS Mode 2**:  \n  - **HDR / Extended Color Gamut workflow**.  \n  - Used for **HDR (High Dynamic Range)** content.  \n  - Applies **linear color space** handling with **extended color gamut** (e.g., Display-P3, Rec.2021).  \n  - **No gamma correction** is applied in the same way; instead, it uses **linear HDR encoding**.  \n  - **Use when**: You are displaying HDR content (e.g., HDR videos, HDR games, or using HDR compositors like `hyprland` with HDR support).\n\n### 2. **When to Use CCS Mode 4?**\n\n**CCS Mode 4** is **not a standard user-selectable mode** in the same way as 1 and "}